Part A: Cryptographic Algorithms
---------------------------------------------

Files:
- cs457_crypto.c / cs457_crypto.h – contains implementations of:
    - One-time pad cipher
    - Affine cipher
    - Substitution decryptor
    - Scytale cipher

- demo.c – demonstrates encryption and decryption for Part A

Notes:
- For the substitution cipher, the final plaintext was manually recovered and included below.
- The scytale implementation uses reinsert_format() to restore punctuation and casing after decryption.

Final Substitution Decryption:

THE WRATH SING, GODDESS, OF PELEUS' SON, ACHILLES, THAT
DESTRUCTIVE WRATH WHICH BROUGHT COUNTLESS WOES UPON THE ACHAEANS, AND SENT
FORTH TO HADES MANY VALIANT SOULS OF HEROES, AND MADE THEM THEMSELVES SPOIL
FOR DOGS AND EVERY BIRD;
its in caps coz of my implementation

$ make 
$ ./demo


Part B: MD5 Cracker
---------------------------------------------

File:
- md5_cracker.c – includes:
    - Dictionary attack using rockyou.txt
    - Brute-force attack (a-z0-9, length ≤ 8)
    - Timing comparison

How to run:
$ make
$ ./md5crack

Hashes tested:
- d077f244def8a70e5ea758bd8352fcd8 cat
- df53ca268240ca76670c8566ee54568a computer
- 6b718641741f992e68ec3712718561b8 Greece

Note: "cat" was found via brute-force faster than dictionary due to its short length.


Part C: RSA Implementation
---------------------------------------------

File:
- rsa.c – RSA key generation, encryption and decryption.

Keys:
- p = 61, q = 53
- n = 3233, φ(n) = 3120
- e = 17
- d = 2753

Encrypts and decrypts small integer messages using:
    c = m^e mod n
    m = c^d mod n

How to run:
$ make
$ ./rsa

all the parts have makefile inside
